Cloud-enabled vs. cloud-native. Remember that distinction? One group lifted old apps onto AWS and called it a day. The other rebuilt from scratch. Looked the same for a year. Five years later you couldn't compare them.

We're doing it again. With AI.

The companies pulling away aren't the ones with the boldest memo or the biggest tool budget. They're the ones treating this as deliberate, sustained work. Not a project. Not an initiative with a finish line. Not something you announce, deploy, and tick off.

Because here's the thing: AI isn't a tool you adopt. It's a way of operating you grow into. It has to end up in the DNA — in how decisions get made, how teams are shaped, how problems get framed before anyone writes a line of code. And DNA doesn't change because someone sent a memo.

The shape of the work is unsexy. You move through bottlenecks in order, and you don't get to skip ahead:

1. Are people actually using the tools? (harder than it sounds)
2. Once they are, are engineers shipping faster?
3. Once they're shipping faster, can the company decide what to build fast enough to keep up?
4. Are you building your own internal tools for all this — and throwing them away when they get stale?
5. And honestly: how should teams even be organized in this new world? Nobody has figured this out yet.

Most companies stall at #3. Engineering turns into a firehose pointed at the wrong target.

One detail worth stealing: at Chainguard, eng managers are expected to sit near the median token usage of their team. Too low and you can't coach. Too high and you're hoarding leverage instead of teaching. Small policy. Says a lot about how seriously they're taking this.

None of this makes for good headlines. The bold memo gets the press. The walkback gets the press. The actual becoming — the messy, deliberate, never-quite-done middle — doesn't.

That's probably how you know it's the part that matters.
